<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Ur</h1><p class="st-meta">2600 BCE – 2112 BCE (approx.) · capital Ur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A rich Sumerian port city of the moon god Nanna, famous for the treasures of its Royal Cemetery and the First Dynasty of Ur.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ur lay near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, close to the shore of the Gulf, and was settled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubaid-period">Ubaid period</a>. In the Early Dynastic III period, around 2600 BCE, it emerged as one of the leading Sumerian cities, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> records a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> of Ur founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesannepadda">Mesannepadda</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ur prospered on trade by sea with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/magan">Magan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meluhha">Meluhha</a>, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, carnelian and lapis lazuli into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-woolley">Leonard Woolley's</a> excavations of 1922 to 1934 uncovered the Royal Cemetery, with the gold helmet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meskalamdug">Meskalamdug</a>, the tomb of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puabi">Puabi</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/standard-of-ur">Standard of Ur</a> and lyres, alongside evidence of attendants buried with their masters. The city's patron was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanna">Nanna</a>, the moon god.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ur fell under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugal-zage-si">Lugal-zage-si</a> and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>. After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gutian-rule">Gutian rule</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> founded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty</a> in 2112 BCE and made Ur the capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>, building its great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a>. The city remained inhabited until about the 4th century BCE, when the river shifted away.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ur.jpg" alt="Ur" width="320" height="362"><figcaption>Image: Steve Harris · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urimki_inscription.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2600 BCE – 2112 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/27th-century-bce">27th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/22nd-century-bce">22nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mesannepadda, A-anepada, Meskalamdug</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sumerian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sumerian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>First Dynasty of Ur, Urim</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2356&amp;focus=ur" aria-label="Ur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5873 1049 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ur lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6193 1305L6129 1232M6193 1305L6193 1305"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6129 1232h0M6193 1305h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6193 1305h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6193 1305h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-2356&amp;focus=ur">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
